 "Agrimson", "firstName": "Barbara", "initials": "B", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Oregon Health & Science University, Portland, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Deville C Jr", "lastName": "Deville", "firstName": "Curtiland", "initials": "C", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-4846-6486"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine, Baltimore, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Rosenthal SA", "lastName": "Rosenthal", "firstName": "Seth A", "initials": "SA", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Sutter Medical Group and Suttter Cancer Center, Sacramento, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Yu JB", "lastName": "Yu", "firstName": "James B", "initials": "JB", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-3119-3226"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Yale University, New Haven, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Thomas CR Jr", "lastName": "Thomas", "firstName": "Charles R", "initials": "CR", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Oregon Health & Science University, Portland, USA."}]}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-5681-7528"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-2122-2003"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-3119-3226"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-5264-3994"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-4846-6486"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics", "supporting_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "3", "volume": "129", "journalIssueId": 2758616, "dateOfPublication": "2018 Dec", "monthOfPublication": 12, "yearOfPublication": 2018, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Radiother Oncol", "title": "Radiotherapy and oncology : journal of the European Society for Therapeutic Radiology and Oncology", "essn": "1879-0887", "issn": "0167-8140", "nlmid": "8407192", "isoabbreviation": "Radiother Oncol"}, "printPublicationDate": "2018-12-01"}, "pubYear": "2018", "pageInfo": "421-426", "abstractText": "Artificial intelligence (AI) is emerging as a technology with the power to transform established industries, and with applications from automated manufacturing to advertising and facial recognition to fully autonomous transportation. Advances in each of these domains have led some to call AI the \"fourth\" industrial revolution [1]. In healthcare, AI is emerging as both a productive and disruptive force across many disciplines. This is perhaps most evident in Diagnostic Radiology and Pathology, specialties largely built around the processing and complex interpretation of medical images, where the role of AI is increasingly seen as both a boon and a threat. In Radiation Oncology as well, AI seems poised to reshape the specialty in significant ways, though the impact of AI has been relatively limited at present, and may rightly seem more distant to many, given the predominantly interpersonal and complex interventional nature of the specialty. In this overview, we will explore the current state and anticipated future impact of AI on Radiation Oncology, in detail, focusing on key topics from multiple stakeholder perspectives, as well as the role our specialty may play in helping to shape the future of AI within the larger spectrum of medicine.", "affiliation": "Oregon Health & Science University, Portland, USA; VA Portland Health Care System, Portland, USA. Electronic address: thompsre@ohsu.edu.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print-Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["research-article", "Review", "Journal Article"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "R01 CA225190", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "R01 CA214825", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Humans"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Patient 